20.Use of final, static, this, and super
	final:
	variable - decalred as constant value cannt be override
	method   - cannot be override
	class 	 - cannot be inherit 
	
	| Keyword    | What It Refers To / Does       | Usage Example            | Key Notes / Memory Tip                                                         |
	| ---------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
	| **final**  | Constant, unchangeable         | `final int x = 10;`      | Cannot change variable, override method, or inherit class. Think **“frozen”**. |
	|            | Method cannot be overridden    | `final void show() {}`   | Protects method from being modified in subclasses.                             |
	|            | Class cannot be subclassed     | `final class A {}`       | Prevents inheritance.                                                          |

	| **static** | Belongs to class, not instance | `static int count = 0;`  | Shared across all objects. Think **“one per class”**.                          |
	|            | Static method                  | `static void greet() {}` | Can call without creating object.                                              |
	|            | Static block                   | `static { … }`           | Runs once when class loads.                                                    |

	| **this**   | Refers to current object       | `this.x = x;`            | Access instance variables or constructors. Think **“me, the current object”**. |
	|            | Call another constructor       | `this(10);`              | Useful for constructor chaining.                                               |
	|            | Pass current object            | `display(this);`         | Often used to pass object reference.                                           |

	| **super**  | Refers to parent class         | `super.x`                | Access parent class field. Think **“above me”**.                               |
	|            | Call parent method             | `super.display();`       | Can call overridden method of parent.                                          |
	|            | Call parent constructor 1st    | `super(100);`            | Must be first line in constructor.                                             |
	
